# Final Review

A high-confidence review framework for determining whether work is ready to ship.

Rather than searching for reasons to rewrite good work, this prompt assumes the deliverable is already high quality and only recommends changes that materially improve the final result.

---

## Purpose

Use this prompt immediately before publishing, submitting, or shipping any important work.

The objective is not perfection.

The objective is to maximize signal while minimizing unnecessary iteration.

---

## Philosophy

Good work should be published.

Every recommendation carries a cost.

The burden of proof is on the reviewer to demonstrate that a change meaningfully improves the deliverable.

Prefer shipping over polishing.

---

## Prompt

```text
Perform one final review of your previous response.

Assume the work is already high quality. Your default assumption should be that it is ready to ship.

Your goal is to identify only changes that would materially improve the final deliverable.

Review for:

• Missing insights or perspectives
• Weak assumptions or unsupported conclusions
• Logical gaps or inconsistencies
• Missing evidence or rationale
• Risks, tradeoffs, or edge cases that should be acknowledged
• Structural issues that materially reduce clarity or usability
• Unnecessary complexity that should be simplified
• Missing connections between ideas
• Higher-leverage alternatives that were overlooked

Rules:

• Optimize for signal over volume.
• Prioritize strategic improvements over stylistic edits.
• Ignore wording tweaks, cosmetic edits, and low-value polish.
• Preserve the original intent and structure unless changing them materially improves the result.
• Do not recommend changes solely for the sake of change.
• If uncertain whether a change clears the bar, do not make it.

Output exactly one of the following:

1. Ship as-is.

OR

2. The complete revised version.

If you output a revised version:

• Apply every meaningful improvement directly.
• Do not explain your reasoning.
• Do not summarize the changes.
• Do not annotate the revisions.
• Do not include commentary before or after.
• Output only the final revised deliverable.
```

---

## Best Use Cases

- Articles
- Case studies
- Strategy documents
- Portfolio artifacts
- README files
- Documentation
- Long-form writing
- Operating systems
- Project deliverables

---

## Design Principles

This framework is built around a few core ideas:

- Default to shipping.
- Optimize for judgment over perfection.
- Recommend fewer, higher-leverage changes.
- Separate meaningful improvements from stylistic preferences.
- Preserve the author's intent unless there is a compelling reason to change it.

